WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:03.529
You started your Presidency in 1992.

2
00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:07.101
Andy and I started doing this show in 1993

3
00:00:07.101 --> 00:00:11.560
and so I have some appreciation for, in that span of time,

4
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.691
how much things have changed.

5
00:00:13.691 --> 00:00:17.709
Now, you were out there campaigning hard last year

6
00:00:17.709 --> 00:00:20.920
at this time, you probably, I'm curious:

7
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.598
did you think you saw a different America

8
00:00:22.598 --> 00:00:24.011
than the one that you had seen

9
00:00:24.011 --> 00:00:26.579
when you were campaigning back in 1992?

10
00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:31.031
Do you think we have profoundly changed since then?

11
00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:34.755
Yes and no, that is I think the good news is,

12
00:00:34.755 --> 00:00:38.547
and I know it's hard to see and was totally unfashionable

13
00:00:38.547 --> 00:00:42.714
to talk about in 2016, but, the country is much more diverse

14
00:00:43.675 --> 00:00:47.842
than it used to be, and I think that's a good thing.

15
00:00:48.723 --> 00:00:51.723
(audience applauds)

16
00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:56.645
We are still, compared to other wealthy countries

17
00:00:56.645 --> 00:01:00.058
relatively young, and having lost it,

18
00:01:00.058 --> 00:01:01.667
I can tell you youth matters.

19
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:02.500
(audience laughs)

20
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.383
That is, the median age of the workforce is a big deal

21
00:01:05.383 --> 00:01:08.748
in terms of the economic potential of a country.

22
00:01:08.748 --> 00:01:13.221
So in a sense, the birth rate of native-born Americans

23
00:01:13.221 --> 00:01:17.932
of all races is just barely at replacement level.

24
00:01:17.932 --> 00:01:20.602
We need immigrants to come in
(audience applauds)

25
00:01:20.602 --> 00:01:24.549
and keep diversifying the country and helping us grow.

26
00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:25.382
(audience cheers and applauds)

27
00:01:25.382 --> 00:01:27.299
But that's better, now.

28
00:01:30.107 --> 00:01:33.357
So the thing that's not so good is that

29
00:01:34.715 --> 00:01:36.589
we're in the middle of--
Trump!

30
00:01:36.589 --> 00:01:38.980
Of revolution, yeah,
(audience laughs)

31
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.337
Well there is that.

32
00:01:40.337 --> 00:01:41.519
(audience laughs)

33
00:01:41.519 --> 00:01:44.250
Excuse me, I'm sorry, sir, we know.

34
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.298
(audience laughs)

35
00:01:47.298 --> 00:01:49.837
Believe me I know, especially today.

36
00:01:49.837 --> 00:01:51.755
But anyway, we're in the middle of this transition

37
00:01:51.755 --> 00:01:54.082
of how we get our information, how we communicate,

38
00:01:54.082 --> 00:01:58.249
and the pattern of economic growth has heavily favored

39
00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:04.525
people with more education and heavily favored

40
00:02:04.525 --> 00:02:06.733
people without regard to their education level

41
00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:10.337
if they live in or around cities that are growth centers.

42
00:02:10.337 --> 00:02:13.337
So for example in the last election,

43
00:02:15.279 --> 00:02:17.668
President Trump won all these rural counties.

44
00:02:17.668 --> 00:02:21.187
They comprised, he got 46% of the vote.

45
00:02:21.187 --> 00:02:25.186
They comprised 36% of the GDP of the country.

46
00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:28.803
The counties Hillary carried comprised 64% of the GDP

47
00:02:28.803 --> 00:02:30.845
of the country, even though the median income

48
00:02:30.845 --> 00:02:32.698
of a Trump voter was slightly higher

49
00:02:32.698 --> 00:02:35.384
than the median income of a Hillary voter.

50
00:02:35.384 --> 00:02:37.371
The people who voted for her were people

51
00:02:37.371 --> 00:02:38.854
who were hopeful about the future,

52
00:02:38.854 --> 00:02:40.042
believed in the assets of the country,

53
00:02:40.042 --> 00:02:42.236
believed we could grow together and go together,

54
00:02:42.236 --> 00:02:44.189
and we're somehow--
(audience applauds)

55
00:02:44.189 --> 00:02:48.356
And we're basically more resistant to the bizarre nature

56
00:02:50.514 --> 00:02:53.365
of the way the election was covered.

57
00:02:53.365 --> 00:02:55.391
They just looked at the debates and they looked

58
00:02:55.391 --> 00:02:56.939
at the conventions and they made a choice,

59
00:02:56.939 --> 00:03:00.272
and so I think that all of us have to do

60
00:03:01.171 --> 00:03:04.254
is no matter how happy some of us are

61
00:03:06.135 --> 00:03:07.940
about what happened yesterday in the elections,

62
00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.690
we have to remember that the founders told us

63
00:03:13.498 --> 00:03:17.001
our job was to create a more perfect union,

64
00:03:17.001 --> 00:03:19.976
so we have to bridge these economic divides,

65
00:03:19.976 --> 00:03:22.635
just like we ask the people on the other side

66
00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:26.108
of the social divide to help us bridge the social divide.

67
00:03:26.108 --> 00:03:28.097
We have to create inclusive economics,

68
00:03:28.097 --> 00:03:31.343
inclusive societies, and we need an inclusive culture.

69
00:03:31.343 --> 00:03:34.335
(audience applauds)

70
00:03:34.335 --> 00:03:35.168
And then,

71
00:03:37.202 --> 00:03:38.394
we need to tell all these people

72
00:03:38.394 --> 00:03:41.078
that just make up stuff on the internet

73
00:03:41.078 --> 00:03:43.817
to knock it off, that we're voting for people

74
00:03:43.817 --> 00:03:45.925
who are going to do jobs, these are jobs,

75
00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.187
and as soon as somebody gets one of these jobs,

76
00:03:48.187 --> 00:03:50.499
you find out pretty quick that the decisions

77
00:03:50.499 --> 00:03:54.694
they make have consequences, that affects people's lives.

78
00:03:54.694 --> 00:03:58.214
And so it matters what you know and even more important,

79
00:03:58.214 --> 00:04:00.848
it matters how you think.

80
00:04:00.848 --> 00:04:02.585
Whether you're conservative or liberal,

81
00:04:02.585 --> 00:04:05.336
do you believe this is an interdependent age,

82
00:04:05.336 --> 00:04:06.832
and we can't escape each other,

83
00:04:06.832 --> 00:04:09.104
then you gotta decide, which is more important,

84
00:04:09.104 --> 00:04:13.210
our interesting differences, or our common humanity,

85
00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.960
and that's the conclusion I draw,

86
00:04:17.593 --> 00:04:20.465
but it is different and it's way better in some ways,

87
00:04:20.465 --> 00:04:23.239
and much more troubling in some ways.

88
00:04:23.239 --> 00:04:26.931
And the dictators' club in the world,

89
00:04:26.931 --> 00:04:30.059
and there's a bunch of 'em you know, they want two things.

90
00:04:30.059 --> 00:04:33.101
They want nuclear weapons because they feel like

91
00:04:33.101 --> 00:04:35.159
they can never be dislodged no matter how much

92
00:04:35.159 --> 00:04:37.539
people hate 'em if they got nuclear power,

93
00:04:37.539 --> 00:04:40.141
and they want to abolish the line between fact and fiction

94
00:04:40.141 --> 00:04:43.895
and truth and lie because they figure if you don't know

95
00:04:43.895 --> 00:04:46.601
what's true and you don't think you can ever know,

96
00:04:46.601 --> 00:04:51.239
that pretty soon everybody will accept the fact

97
00:04:51.239 --> 00:04:53.827
that democracy's no longer possible.

98
00:04:53.827 --> 00:04:55.761
Right, you're talking about foreign countries now,

99
00:04:55.761 --> 00:04:57.667
or here, 'cause that's (laughing)

100
00:04:57.667 --> 00:04:59.045
(audience laughs)

101
00:04:59.045 --> 00:05:02.291
That shook me to the bone for a second,

102
00:05:02.291 --> 00:05:04.541
I thought we were in Korea.

103
00:05:09.036 --> 00:05:10.000
(audience laughs)

104
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.570
Look, all I can tell you is--
You just said a lot

105
00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:16.854
by saying nothing.
No, but look at the effort

106
00:05:16.854 --> 00:05:20.322
here to disenfranchised voters, to make it harder

107
00:05:20.322 --> 00:05:22.294
for young people, for people of color,

108
00:05:22.294 --> 00:05:26.507
for the elderly, infirm, who are poor and vulnerable,

109
00:05:26.507 --> 00:05:28.396
for all kinds of people to vote.

110
00:05:28.396 --> 00:05:32.128
The reason there's no immigration reform

111
00:05:32.128 --> 00:05:35.325
is because the people who are against it are afraid

112
00:05:35.325 --> 00:05:38.282
it would adversely affect their electoral prospects,

113
00:05:38.282 --> 00:05:42.501
if more people who work and pay taxes could also vote.

114
00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:44.231
And when the Supreme Court gutted the Voting Rights Act,

115
00:05:44.231 --> 00:05:46.867
they made a terrible mistake because it unleashed

116
00:05:46.867 --> 00:05:51.034
all these efforts to basically try to shrink the electorate.

117
00:05:52.275 --> 00:05:54.039
But those of you who don't like it

118
00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:56.133
should own some responsibility

119
00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:59.977
if you have ever missed a midterm election.

120
00:05:59.977 --> 00:06:01.099
(audience applauds)
Because that's when

121
00:06:01.099 --> 00:06:04.228
most of the governors are elected, that's mhen most

122
00:06:04.228 --> 00:06:07.618
of the secretary or the legislators are elected,

123
00:06:07.618 --> 00:06:09.897
so we all got to own up, we need to just go out

124
00:06:09.897 --> 00:06:13.705
and start going up to people who are mad at us

125
00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:15.302
and say, "Look let's get over it,

126
00:06:15.302 --> 00:06:16.308
"we've got to live together,

127
00:06:16.308 --> 00:06:18.581
"we've got to share this together and if we work together,

128
00:06:18.581 --> 00:06:21.680
"we'll have better economics, we'll have better societies,

129
00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.955
"and we'll have a culture that we can all be a part of."

130
00:06:23.955 --> 00:06:27.955
(audience applauds and cheers)

